<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter13_polymorphism_and_dynamic_dispatch">
    <title>Polymorphism and Dynamic Dispatch</title>

    <p>
        <term>Polymorphism</term> means that one interface can work with objects of
        different derived types. When a virtual member function is called through a
        base reference or pointer, C++ selects the implementation belonging to the
        actual object. This run-time selection is called <term>dynamic dispatch</term>.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_base_references_and_pointers">
        <title>Base References and Pointers</title>

        <p>
            A base reference or pointer can refer to a derived object. The expression
            used to call the virtual function can remain the same while the actual
            object determines which implementation runs.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter13_dynamic_dispatch">
            <title>A base reference invokes derived behavior</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter13_dynamic_dispatch"
                     language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Shape2D {
public:
    virtual void report_type() const {
        std::cout << "A two-dimensional shape\n";
    }

    virtual ~Shape2D() = default;
};

class Circle : public Shape2D {
public:
    void report_type() const override {
        std::cout << "A circle\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Circle circle;
    Shape2D& shape_reference = circle;

    shape_reference.report_type();

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The expression <c>shape_reference.report_type()</c> is written using the
            base interface, but the call executes <c>Circle::report_type()</c>.
            The reference does not copy the circle; it refers to the existing object.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_object_slicing">
        <title>Object Slicing</title>

        <p>
            Passing a derived object by value to a function whose parameter is the
            base type creates a new base object. The derived part is left out. This
            loss of the derived portion is called <term>object slicing</term>.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter13_object_slicing">
            <title>Passing by value slices the derived object</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter13_object_slicing"
                     language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Shape2D {
public:
    virtual void report_type() const {
        std::cout << "A two-dimensional shape\n";
    }

    virtual ~Shape2D() = default;
};

class Circle : public Shape2D {
public:
    void report_type() const override {
        std::cout << "A circle\n";
    }
};

void report_shape_by_value(Shape2D shape) {
    shape.report_type();
}

int main() {
    Circle circle;
    report_shape_by_value(circle);

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The function receives a separate <c>Shape2D</c> object, so the
            <c>Circle</c> portion is sliced away. Passing a base reference or pointer
            preserves the complete derived object and allows dynamic dispatch.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_shape2d_polymorphic_array">
        <title>A Polymorphic Array of Two-Dimensional Shapes</title>

        <p>
            The following example stores circles, rectangles, ellipses, and triangles
            in one <c>std::array</c>. The array stores pointers to existing objects;
            it does not store the objects by value and does not own their lifetimes.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter13_shape2d_polymorphic_array">
            <title>One array can use the common Shape2D interface</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter13_shape2d_polymorphic_array"
                     language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <array>
#include <iostream>

class Shape2D {
public:
    virtual double calculate_area() const = 0;
    virtual ~Shape2D() = default;
};

class Circle : public Shape2D {
private:
    double radius;

public:
    Circle(double starting_radius)
        : radius{starting_radius} {
    }

    double calculate_area() const override {
        return 3.14159 * radius * radius;
    }
};

class Rectangle : public Shape2D {
private:
    double width;
    double height;

public:
    Rectangle(double starting_width, double starting_height)
        : width{starting_width}, height{starting_height} {
    }

    double calculate_area() const override {
        return width * height;
    }
};

class Ellipse : public Shape2D {
private:
    double semi_major_axis;
    double semi_minor_axis;

public:
    Ellipse(double major_axis, double minor_axis)
        : semi_major_axis{major_axis}, semi_minor_axis{minor_axis} {
    }

    double calculate_area() const override {
        return 3.14159 * semi_major_axis * semi_minor_axis;
    }
};

class Triangle : public Shape2D {
private:
    double base;
    double height;

public:
    Triangle(double starting_base, double starting_height)
        : base{starting_base}, height{starting_height} {
    }

    double calculate_area() const override {
        return 0.5 * base * height;
    }
};

int main() {
    Circle circle{2.0};
    Rectangle rectangle{3.0, 4.0};
    Ellipse ellipse{3.0, 2.0};
    Triangle triangle{3.0, 4.0};

    std::array<const Shape2D*, 4> shapes{
        &circle, &rectangle, &ellipse, &triangle
    };

    for (const Shape2D* shape : shapes) {
        std::cout << shape->calculate_area() << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            A declaration such as <c>std::array&lt;Shape2D, 4&gt;</c> would not work
            here because <c>Shape2D</c> is abstract. Even when a base class is
            concrete, storing derived objects by value can slice away their derived
            portions. The pointer array preserves the complete objects and lets the
            virtual call select the correct area calculation.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_virtual_destructors">
        <title>Why Polymorphic Bases Need Virtual Destructors</title>

        <p>
            A base class used polymorphically should normally have a virtual
            destructor. This ensures that deleting a derived object through a base
            pointer runs the derived destructor before the base destructor. The
            <c>Shape2D</c> examples therefore declare
            <c>virtual ~Shape2D() = default;</c>.
        </p>

        <p>
            The shape array above uses pointers to local objects, so it does not call
            <c>delete</c>. The virtual destructor is still part of the correct
            polymorphic base-class interface for situations involving dynamically
            created derived objects.
        </p>

        <p>
            The following visualization shows dynamic dispatch through a base
            reference before the complete shape-array example is studied.
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive_chapter13_polymorphism"
                     iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%0Aclass%20Shape2D%20%7B%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20virtual%20void%20report_type%28%29%20const%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22A%20two-dimensional%20shape%5Cn%22%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%0A%20%20%20%20virtual%20~Shape2D%28%29%20%3D%20default%3B%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aclass%20Circle%20%3A%20public%20Shape2D%20%7B%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20void%20report_type%28%29%20const%20override%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22A%20circle%5Cn%22%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20Circle%20circle%3B%0A%20%20%20%20Shape2D%26%20shape_reference%20%3D%20circle%3B%0A%20%20%20%20shape_reference.report_type%28%29%3B%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=360&amp;codeDivWidth=420&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;curInstr=0&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp_g%2B%2B9.3.0"
                     width="100%"
                     aspect="16:10">
        </interactive>
    </subsection>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises_chapter13_polymorphism">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_polymorphism_1">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Why does the shape example use
                    <c>std::array&lt;const Shape2D*, 4&gt;</c> instead of
                    <c>std::array&lt;Shape2D, 4&gt;</c>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Pointers are always faster than objects.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>The important issue is preserving different derived object types.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p><c>Shape2D</c> is abstract, and pointers preserve the complete derived objects.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>The array stores addresses while virtual calls use each object's implementation.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p><c>std::array</c> can store only pointers.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p><c>std::array</c> can store objects of many types, including class objects.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Derived classes cannot contain data members.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Derived classes can contain their own data members.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_polymorphism_multiple">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which statements about polymorphism are correct? Select all
                    that apply.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>A base reference can refer to a derived object.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>This allows a common interface to work with derived objects.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>A virtual call can select a derived implementation at run time.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>This selection is dynamic dispatch.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Passing a derived object by base value preserves every derived member.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Passing by value can cause object slicing.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>A polymorphic base class should generally have a virtual destructor.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>This supports correct destruction through a base pointer.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_polymorphism_2">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is object slicing?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    Object slicing occurs when a derived object is copied into a base
                    object by value, leaving out the derived portion.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_polymorphism_parsons"
                  number="yes"
                  adaptive="yes"
                  indentation="show">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Arrange the blocks to create an array of pointers to four
                    existing <c>Shape2D</c> objects.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <blocks randomize="yes">
                <block order="1">
                    <cline>std::array&lt;const Shape2D*, 4&gt; shapes{</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="2">
                    <cline>    &amp;circle, &amp;rectangle, &amp;ellipse, &amp;triangle</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="3">
                    <cline>};</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="1" correct="no">
                    <cline>std::array&lt;Shape2D, 4&gt; shapes{</cline>
                </block>
            </blocks>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>
</section>
